 {:db/id "q-80496/ma-source-field/b2b50d05c73d24451e335db151425ad04b8f7524ae2c41dcfb10f733011507c4" :answer-field/id #uuid "5324a7ce-971d-53cb-b107-19a127a08e1f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-80496/ma-source-field/b2b50d05c73d24451e335db151425ad04b8f7524ae2c41dcfb10f733011507c4/2caf8873a0b6230d1da4479e09489513e971f6209e4cc0bf9afd97773ef077b2" "q-80496/ma-source-field/b2b50d05c73d24451e335db151425ad04b8f7524ae2c41dcfb10f733011507c4/40427b9cbd0565dc5f2edc1baba0772e73f40d14ec72853aac37aaf6d29f3f98" "q-80496/ma-source-field/b2b50d05c73d24451e335db151425ad04b8f7524ae2c41dcfb10f733011507c4/a355e3c0dfab0ef37ad17b2b76567b8939ecb537de3710525c1952699a9836af" "q-80496/ma-source-field/b2b50d05c73d24451e335db151425ad04b8f7524ae2c41dcfb10f733011507c4/7cdc8035c8b1df75a1409828e52096ae672f4cc77076bd8624b23e5333467812" "q-80496/ma-source-field/b2b50d05c73d24451e335db151425ad04b8f7524ae2c41dcfb10f733011507c4/19f4178242e734b359d0168f8d1238bc50b3bb545c4425ea335eeffff0d1cf39"] :answer-field/correct "q-80496/ma-source-field/b2b50d05c73d24451e335db151425ad04b8f7524ae2c41dcfb10f733011507c4/a355e3c0dfab0ef37ad17b2b76567b8939ecb537de3710525c1952699a9836af" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-80496" :question/id #uuid "f53e1d4c-a53d-5c3e-8ae8-0e080a8e331f" :question/math-academy-id "q-80496" :question/problem "Find matrices $P$ and $D$ such that $PD{P}^{-1}=B,$ where $D$ is a diagonal matrix and $B=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 3 \\\\ -6 & 4\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the diagonalization algorithm.\n\n**Step 1.** We need to find the eigenvalues of the matrix $B$ using its characteristic equation $det(B-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}-5-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ -6 & 4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-5-\\lambda )(4-\\lambda )-3\\cdot (-6) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+\\lambda -2 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda +2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the eigenvalues of $B$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2.$ \n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $B.$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-I & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 3 \\\\ -6 & 4\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 3 \\\\ -6 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-6 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}.$  Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=2,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-(-2)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 3 \\\\ -6 & 4\\end{aligned}]-(-2)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 \\\\ -6 & 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to its row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -6 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & ={R}_{2}+(-2){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}],\\,P=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-80496/ma-source-field/b2b50d05c73d24451e335db151425ad04b8f7524ae2c41dcfb10f733011507c4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
