 {:db/id "q-80825/ma-source-field/34b911ad60d4878b359e9329e94824f25218aa553e3eae65fa96a402e2472037" :answer-field/id #uuid "135ac5a3-acd1-57d7-85af-ac59d7bb8214" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-80825/ma-source-field/34b911ad60d4878b359e9329e94824f25218aa553e3eae65fa96a402e2472037/73ff2449378b2e1793f2cf309c230f993426ab33a12e722b39978a1c56a39685" "q-80825/ma-source-field/34b911ad60d4878b359e9329e94824f25218aa553e3eae65fa96a402e2472037/2476ebf9ee3010db6342843e58ff4ae506b921ee1a00e970c6abfb1c7fb9325d" "q-80825/ma-source-field/34b911ad60d4878b359e9329e94824f25218aa553e3eae65fa96a402e2472037/ed58388be20aa7bfac211a1fa09210bc6f5a6778a089d042e0a470fdb373ad8d" "q-80825/ma-source-field/34b911ad60d4878b359e9329e94824f25218aa553e3eae65fa96a402e2472037/0a44ea7392d1c976ae683a99088b193d1a3fa24879999646752d32bae4917760" "q-80825/ma-source-field/34b911ad60d4878b359e9329e94824f25218aa553e3eae65fa96a402e2472037/4afd66b6a207a8fabb431b33d809c1913d1338d877c7186594eaf9c610c1596d"] :answer-field/correct "q-80825/ma-source-field/34b911ad60d4878b359e9329e94824f25218aa553e3eae65fa96a402e2472037/73ff2449378b2e1793f2cf309c230f993426ab33a12e722b39978a1c56a39685" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-80825" :question/id #uuid "ba3b9cc6-841d-5636-9777-0bc1e8c24e7f" :question/math-academy-id "q-80825" :question/problem "Determine all real eigenvalues $\\lambda $ of the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -1\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the matrix $\\,A-\\lambda I:$\n﻿﻿﻿\n\n$$\nA-\\lambda I=[\\begin{aligned}-1-\\lambda  & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation is given by $det(A-\\lambda I)=0.$ \n\nExpanding the determinant across the $1$st row, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-1-\\lambda  & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 2-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-1-\\lambda )|\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}|-1|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-{\\lambda }^{3}+6\\lambda +5)-(-1-\\lambda ) & =0 \\\\ -{\\lambda }^{3}+7\\lambda +6 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{3}-7\\lambda -6 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to solve this equation. We find the first root by testing some values. According to the rational zeros theorem, a list of good first guesses consists of all divisors of $6:$ \n\n$$\n\\pm 1,\\pm 2,\\pm 3,\\pm 6\n$$\n\n- Let's pick $x=1.$ Substituting to the characteristic equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1)^{3}-7(1)-6 & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\lambda =1$ is not a root of the equation.\n\n- Let's pick $x=-1.$ Substituting to the characteristic equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-1)^{3}-7(-1)-6 & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $\\lambda =-1$ is a root of the equation.\n\nSo, we can factor $(\\lambda +1)$ out of ${\\lambda }^{3}-7\\lambda -6$ using synthetic division:\n\n| $-1$ | $1$ | $0$ | $-7$ | $-6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | $↓$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $6$ |\n|  | $1$ | $-1$ | $-6$ | $0$ |\n\nSo, we obtain the following factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\lambda +1)({\\lambda }^{2}-\\lambda -6) & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)(\\lambda +2)(\\lambda -3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-1,$ $\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-2,\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{3}=3.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-80825/ma-source-field/34b911ad60d4878b359e9329e94824f25218aa553e3eae65fa96a402e2472037"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
