 {:db/id "q-81202" :question/id #uuid "225d5964-b5f6-5f9c-9606-7856be911138" :question/math-academy-id "q-81202" :question/problem "Determine the horizontal asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{3+4{x}^{2}-2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}-x}.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by the dominant term in the denominator, and then we let $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIgnoring the coefficient, the dominant term in the denominator is ${x}^{2}.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by ${x}^{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{3+4{x}^{2}-2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}-x} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}})}{(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}+4-2x)}{(1-\\frac{1}{x})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, when $x$ is very large, the terms $\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}$ and $\\frac{1}{x}$ become very small. Therefore, as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}}+4-2x)}{(1-\\frac{1}{x})} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{0+4-2x}{1-0} \\\\  & =4-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is not a constant, which means that $y=f(x)$ does not have a horizontal asymptote." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-81202/ma-source-field/a4c3604bbf95a2f29e270877e8c7902346813f03aed222fb844953a6598e0355"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
