 {:db/id "q-81236" :question/id #uuid "811972db-6534-593a-aa1e-100258f3cf1f" :question/math-academy-id "q-81236" :question/problem "Find the eigenvectors of the $2\\times 2$ matrix $T$ given that \n\n$$\nT[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{2} & 2 \\\\ 2 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{2} & 2 \\\\ 2 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{2} & 2 \\\\ 2 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]$ appears on both sides of the equation. Since this matrix is invertible (its determinant is not zero), right-multiplying the equation by the inverse gives the following expression for $T:$\n\n$$\nT={{[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{2} & 2 \\\\ 2 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{2} & 2 \\\\ 2 & \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{-1}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $T$ is diagonalizable. The eigenvalues of $T$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=\\frac{1}{2}$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1$ and the corresponding eigenvectors are the columns of the matrix $P:$\n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\sqrt{2} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}]$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-81236/ma-source-field/91a825843ba0783f4471a98222369bf1dd4d84c3db9a42669030fe323b6e19ba"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
 {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "237e9db9-a621-5abe-be24-d11ec63840d5"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-81236"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-81273/ma-source-field/59e16963b65c60409fc6874af3d7f6648835b6c4dc4b07e4bef3635edc98cd58/e46b04aeb2531655daf502b0bbd65c2a0f614d9d96f8b84f5ebe210db6df752f" :answer/id #uuid "1669bc78-1efd-58f9-91e9-a08a66025cf0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "The critical points lie on the line $u=0$ and on the parabola $u=4{v}^{2}.$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]}
