 {:db/id "q-81324" :question/id #uuid "5883242f-b44f-5ff7-9fa7-30bcd180241a" :question/math-academy-id "q-81324" :question/problem "Consider the standard basis ${i,j}$ of ${R}^{2}.$ If $T$ is a linear transformation such that $T(i)=\\frac{1}{2}i$ and $T(j)=j,$ then what is the geometric interpretation of the transformation $T?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's consider the unit square spanned by the vectors $i$ and  $j.$\n\n![](images/34/34c82826da26290fc890671734c354fbfd9c253f1cfaca38de1ea615434e6766.png)\n\nNow, using the fact that $T(i)=\\frac{1}{2}i$ and $T(j)=j,$ we can draw the image of the square under the transformation $T:$\n\n![](images/02/02470083dea4808af1854e9ed88ab496828a429c593af780209d8a6d4b15a6b1.png)\n\nTherefore, the linear transformation $T$ represents a stretch with a scale factor $\\frac{1}{2}$ in the $x$-direction." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-81324/ma-source-field/271464cd66b6d29ce0b22f82aa433d4ddcec44ecabc904e88582707655da7be0"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
