 {:db/id "q-83217/ma-source-field/a628317d2db8e0a207cb410ff2aa3f7ffad4801712bf0f44b818822bb67448d1" :answer-field/id #uuid "62f9d6d4-3621-5ba8-8abe-069970462f3e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-83217/ma-source-field/a628317d2db8e0a207cb410ff2aa3f7ffad4801712bf0f44b818822bb67448d1/2090a7507318debb52c1ba597b07efc360a06b0d45f14544f205b7f1f431ee2b" "q-83217/ma-source-field/a628317d2db8e0a207cb410ff2aa3f7ffad4801712bf0f44b818822bb67448d1/0a674132d74042691d52aecd3d6a949a70bdc7ca10dbafaa440d4a1689ebd5fb" "q-83217/ma-source-field/a628317d2db8e0a207cb410ff2aa3f7ffad4801712bf0f44b818822bb67448d1/d7871a3e89eef25c34b28e8c19f92c64faa39edcf5065edc2f600d831ba2fb1c" "q-83217/ma-source-field/a628317d2db8e0a207cb410ff2aa3f7ffad4801712bf0f44b818822bb67448d1/cb8754a24d74967cfc293f77d78d076ce1cfee0c0dee7e19eafefdccacb22f3c" "q-83217/ma-source-field/a628317d2db8e0a207cb410ff2aa3f7ffad4801712bf0f44b818822bb67448d1/99f838ef9ebe3602f29c32de00011e4bb9894a60dc105256174e68c39ff6a926"] :answer-field/correct "q-83217/ma-source-field/a628317d2db8e0a207cb410ff2aa3f7ffad4801712bf0f44b818822bb67448d1/0a674132d74042691d52aecd3d6a949a70bdc7ca10dbafaa440d4a1689ebd5fb" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-83217" :question/id #uuid "f750b509-3c63-5fc0-8993-9bde234b6a4e" :question/math-academy-id "q-83217" :question/problem "Use the first two non-zero terms of the Taylor expansion of ${x}^{-1/2}$ about $x=9$ to approximate the value of ${8}^{-1/2}.$" :question/worked-solution "We use Taylor's formula in the form\n\n$$\nf(x)=f(a)+{f}^{′}(a)(x-a)+\\frac{{f}^{″}(a)}{2!}(x-a)^{2}+⋯,\n$$\n\nwith $a=9.$ \n\nCalculating the necessary derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{-1/2} \\\\ f(9) & ={9}^{-1/2}=\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{-3/2} \\\\ {f}^{′}(9) & =-\\frac{1}{2}(9)^{-3/2}=-\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 27}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the Taylor series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{-1/2} & =\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{2\\cdot 27}(x-9)+⋯ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{x-9}{54}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute $x=8$ into the above to approximate ${8}^{-1/2}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{8}^{-1/2} & ≈\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{8-9}{54} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{54} \\\\  & =\\frac{19}{54}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-83217/ma-source-field/a628317d2db8e0a207cb410ff2aa3f7ffad4801712bf0f44b818822bb67448d1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
