 {:db/id "q-83429" :question/id #uuid "83cee5be-d033-528b-aa1a-c2f13225ee96" :question/math-academy-id "q-83429" :question/problem "Find the first three non-zero terms of the Maclaurin expansion of ${x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$" :question/worked-solution "The standard Maclaurin expansion for $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =x-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3!}+\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5!}-⋯ \\\\  & =x-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}+\\frac{{x}^{5}}{120}-⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo get an expansion for ${x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ we multiply this series by ${x}^{2}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & ={x}^{2}(x-\\frac{{x}^{3}}{6}+\\frac{{x}^{5}}{120}-⋯) \\\\  & ={x}^{3}-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{6}+\\frac{{x}^{7}}{120}-⋯\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-83429/ma-source-field/b674640bf5a07337b0a2ee3cd251271c7472ddfa7049dae8cda13080561fe9d4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
