 {:db/id "q-8369/ma-source-field/bc28bf26e012c6db5e4a3b0a79ef9baa71c21110628f548f898c454f9a25375d" :answer-field/id #uuid "b19317f0-552c-5bb1-aa73-1ed888b17d05" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-8369/ma-source-field/bc28bf26e012c6db5e4a3b0a79ef9baa71c21110628f548f898c454f9a25375d/80515c47759e01fffdff039bbf6bad3c411e4447dc98ae0140749030d185b3ce" "q-8369/ma-source-field/bc28bf26e012c6db5e4a3b0a79ef9baa71c21110628f548f898c454f9a25375d/3d4413e86f4150db897292b00eaede004b3601daaf453ec545f3bed3aa86b4fb" "q-8369/ma-source-field/bc28bf26e012c6db5e4a3b0a79ef9baa71c21110628f548f898c454f9a25375d/aa2714b7d3dec6193b14ee35d452a59cd94a6882454adb59a60dd84bcb2b094f" "q-8369/ma-source-field/bc28bf26e012c6db5e4a3b0a79ef9baa71c21110628f548f898c454f9a25375d/15901b89f31f4fa938a35662930fd6ae65d79308a89985b525593942d133a463" "q-8369/ma-source-field/bc28bf26e012c6db5e4a3b0a79ef9baa71c21110628f548f898c454f9a25375d/6c94c42d1f90906af92b16efa8285624e42775ffe2c22ca3752a4c9c955cb8fd"] :answer-field/correct "q-8369/ma-source-field/bc28bf26e012c6db5e4a3b0a79ef9baa71c21110628f548f898c454f9a25375d/6c94c42d1f90906af92b16efa8285624e42775ffe2c22ca3752a4c9c955cb8fd" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-8369" :question/id #uuid "29260781-8760-5b93-aff1-74357dc9fb2c" :question/math-academy-id "q-8369" :question/problem "Which of the following is a first-order homogeneous ordinary differential equation?" :question/worked-solution "A first-order ordinary differential equation that is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y)\n$$\n\nis said to be homogeneous if it satisfies the following property:\n\n$$\nf(tx,ty)=f(x,y)\\,\n$$\n\nThe only option in which this is the case is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe can check this using one of two methods.\n\n**Method 1**\n\nLet $f(x,y)=\\frac{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}}.$\n\nWe see that both the numerator and denominator are homogeneous polynomials of degree $2.$ Therefore, $f(x,y)$ is a homogeneous function of degree $2-2=0.$\n\nHence, the original differential equation is homogeneous.\n\n**Method 2**\n\nTo check this in detail using the definition, we first write:\n\n$$\nf(x,y)=\\frac{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}}\n$$\n\nWe can check that this is homogeneous showing that $f(tx,ty)=f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =\\frac{(ty)^{2}-(tx)^{2}}{(ty)^{2}+(tx)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}{y}^{2}-{t}^{2}{x}^{2}}{{t}^{2}{y}^{2}+{t}^{2}{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}({y}^{2}-{x}^{2})}{{t}^{2}({y}^{2}+{x}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{2}-{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}+{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $f(tx,ty)=f(x,y),$ the original differential equation is homogeneous." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-8369/ma-source-field/bc28bf26e012c6db5e4a3b0a79ef9baa71c21110628f548f898c454f9a25375d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
 {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e8c2a855-07a3-5855-9f6c-1a914953ac53"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-8369"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-83710/ma-source-field/3d1001e0ee25246968d61268a1506960d91721be2af6ae9ac55b14ca8ee9447d/b97222231d3f6ff9b3d89a8051afddfc52a908c25850b5827b61346b3a762cdc" :answer/id #uuid "58ff7c16-1b9d-5ada-98a5-763a6cfe2824" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$D=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ and  $P=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]}
