 {:db/id "q-84048" :question/id #uuid "3982bc28-35e9-548b-a313-78e4ed431710" :question/math-academy-id "q-84048" :question/problem "Calculate the $y$-intercepts of the hyperbola $(x-3)^{2}-\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{2}=1.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the $y$-intercepts of the hyperbola, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the hyperbola and solve for $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2}-\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ (0-3)^{2}-\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ 9-\\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(y+2)^{2}}{2} & =8 \\\\ (y+2)^{2} & =16 \\\\ y+2 & =\\pm 4 \\\\ y & =-6,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercepts are $(0,-6)$ and $(0,2).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-84048/ma-source-field/6233a7e20fdbc1f50a4e6349c774cba87a2f02ac759b8368333edcbf1f14a7dd"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
