 {:db/id "q-85643/ma-source-field/3cff98017ede886a2a172023f52140fa4c2ecdbd5e78f8ca224e679d762b790f" :answer-field/id #uuid "e48430d0-09a9-535a-843b-f4fdfbee498f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-85643/ma-source-field/3cff98017ede886a2a172023f52140fa4c2ecdbd5e78f8ca224e679d762b790f/2ddda6672cd9117c496b40039bb24e9551b86514a0ced6b2d869664914795cff" "q-85643/ma-source-field/3cff98017ede886a2a172023f52140fa4c2ecdbd5e78f8ca224e679d762b790f/250d160b15e9359628e412b91e5c1fd679c52d57a82017145d130d6c7fa4646e" "q-85643/ma-source-field/3cff98017ede886a2a172023f52140fa4c2ecdbd5e78f8ca224e679d762b790f/02bd85d8ef0eab243b5263b64b9ff5a329de46d7086d62c76e90e0b6d5401f96" "q-85643/ma-source-field/3cff98017ede886a2a172023f52140fa4c2ecdbd5e78f8ca224e679d762b790f/a3d13a5807221d895018d7b06558d926d62bfb001b459077c27b527e0d873fba" "q-85643/ma-source-field/3cff98017ede886a2a172023f52140fa4c2ecdbd5e78f8ca224e679d762b790f/fbc9f6433e70c687e4cd524a01759124f3a0b394750ef110b890774618ad4f45"] :answer-field/correct "q-85643/ma-source-field/3cff98017ede886a2a172023f52140fa4c2ecdbd5e78f8ca224e679d762b790f/02bd85d8ef0eab243b5263b64b9ff5a329de46d7086d62c76e90e0b6d5401f96" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-85643" :question/id #uuid "0902cd66-9d18-50eb-a894-3b1edfc4b17b" :question/math-academy-id "q-85643" :question/problem "![](images/3b/3be6fa94deb7fc38d870e5a31cc81c3cfd6b9ab84c21340e7e88f7246b1058bc.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $\\frac{5}{7},$ what is the value of $sec⁡\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/17/17e6320115f2cf8b5ffad6abc099169774536af20d2559b453aaa97c1286d96d.png)\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{5}{7}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{5}{7}.$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin⁡\\theta =\\frac{5}{7}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos⁡\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{5}{7})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{25}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{24}{49}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{24}}{7} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the negative answer since $cos⁡\\theta $ is negative in the second quadrant. So, $cos⁡\\theta =-\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{7}.$\n\nFinally, knowing that $sec⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{cos⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\nsec⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{(-\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{7})}=-\\frac{7}{2\\sqrt{6}}=-\\frac{7\\sqrt{6}}{12}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-85643/ma-source-field/3cff98017ede886a2a172023f52140fa4c2ecdbd5e78f8ca224e679d762b790f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
