 {:db/id "q-86709/ma-source-field/b710a07b94562dd60a625a1d5798c81e7ac22d6de279699b08fc56463600729a" :answer-field/id #uuid "c1c6b9bc-2cad-5aef-8fbc-0df5f811dfbb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-86709/ma-source-field/b710a07b94562dd60a625a1d5798c81e7ac22d6de279699b08fc56463600729a/0489075ccd4781e59a9040be5b67cee17cf9d7b1b903594c0a16be41a8b63bb4" "q-86709/ma-source-field/b710a07b94562dd60a625a1d5798c81e7ac22d6de279699b08fc56463600729a/1711bdfea17393ae46d9e78791a878b630e5972101f53f130606ddb0908cbec8" "q-86709/ma-source-field/b710a07b94562dd60a625a1d5798c81e7ac22d6de279699b08fc56463600729a/7a51d973a1e3c71d95345010c37232b55bc2bd6101be2884f083f23eb7c84277" "q-86709/ma-source-field/b710a07b94562dd60a625a1d5798c81e7ac22d6de279699b08fc56463600729a/2363230d06ac594e2e7984d3a705f7070b3b0fcfb583f0458fa3f17115662e57" "q-86709/ma-source-field/b710a07b94562dd60a625a1d5798c81e7ac22d6de279699b08fc56463600729a/2c346d9fb2f94b2e74523ee40c3077a5219c64702281fb0a4ec762d517d3e89f"] :answer-field/correct "q-86709/ma-source-field/b710a07b94562dd60a625a1d5798c81e7ac22d6de279699b08fc56463600729a/2363230d06ac594e2e7984d3a705f7070b3b0fcfb583f0458fa3f17115662e57" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-86709" :question/id #uuid "32f5ebff-a024-5f63-b943-142f3a504252" :question/math-academy-id "q-86709" :question/problem "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)=2xy+6x-3$ on the closed region $D$ enclosed by $y=-{x}^{2}$ and $y=-4.$ You are given that $f$ has a critical point at $(0,-3),$ and on the boundary of the region the points that could give rise to extreme values are $(-2,-4),$ $(-1,-1),$ $(1,-1),$ and $(2,-4).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points inside the region $D.$ \n\nTo determine the critical points, let's first sketch the region:\n\n![](images/96/96096b5f8944785f1af1bbc4fd4942e081b4b3999887c0fa8d5d76008af454d5.png)\n\nSince the point $(0,-3)$ is in the interior of $D,$ we include it in the list of candidates for extrema.\n\n**Step 2.** Find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values.\n\nIn this case, we are *given* the points on the boundary of our region that could potentially be global extrema for $f.$ These are\n\n$$\n(-2,-4),\\,(-1,-1),\\,(1,-1),\\,(2,-4).\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0,-3) & =2(0)(-3)+6(0)-3 \\\\  & =-3 \\\\ f(-2,-4) & =2(-2)(-4)+6(-2)-3 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ f(-1,-1) & =2(-1)(-1)+6(-1)-3 \\\\  & =-7 \\\\ f(1,-1) & =2(1)(-1)+6(1)-3 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ f(2,-4) & =2(2)(-4)+6(2)-3 \\\\  & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-7$ at the points $(-1,-1)$ and $(2,-4),$\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $1$ at the points $(-2,-4)$ and $(1,-1).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-86709/ma-source-field/b710a07b94562dd60a625a1d5798c81e7ac22d6de279699b08fc56463600729a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
