 {:db/id "q-86712/ma-source-field/316d1813f8cd52caa135b1a4ad50d41be0b5b5ff23dd6905bb682694f02b2c68" :answer-field/id #uuid "cd6de7a6-023f-5e00-9b0c-d361277d5ae6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-86712/ma-source-field/316d1813f8cd52caa135b1a4ad50d41be0b5b5ff23dd6905bb682694f02b2c68/5fb2f1dff91916d39d626bdb76f4bb252dc39eb96b8fe66e1988c12019624d69" "q-86712/ma-source-field/316d1813f8cd52caa135b1a4ad50d41be0b5b5ff23dd6905bb682694f02b2c68/7afea7ce19c4c35ea4ae4141ed39a52906dabb4aad88b509f41032ec17c6c44e" "q-86712/ma-source-field/316d1813f8cd52caa135b1a4ad50d41be0b5b5ff23dd6905bb682694f02b2c68/1a2fc909ef8ba497cb75b93ecf3d44001142ba11ea67c82ed6e40691eca8041e" "q-86712/ma-source-field/316d1813f8cd52caa135b1a4ad50d41be0b5b5ff23dd6905bb682694f02b2c68/d7812f04867a6fc6467fcdce853c52872bf34e95b92a4e396dff7bf946696e9d" "q-86712/ma-source-field/316d1813f8cd52caa135b1a4ad50d41be0b5b5ff23dd6905bb682694f02b2c68/4cbd78b90502c389eb3ad4875afd77a6064bcb3bb7671a21cad10f2ddda0035b"] :answer-field/correct "q-86712/ma-source-field/316d1813f8cd52caa135b1a4ad50d41be0b5b5ff23dd6905bb682694f02b2c68/5fb2f1dff91916d39d626bdb76f4bb252dc39eb96b8fe66e1988c12019624d69" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-86712" :question/id #uuid "25973738-10c4-5a96-a292-a5b1bfa48a02" :question/math-academy-id "q-86712" :question/problem "![](images/20/20f279f5936987374965cbba01e7e089d88dae1c62767b1ab8eb9ebaea0e5ce9.png)\n\nFind the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)=6x-2xy$ on the closed region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le y\\le 2-{x}^{2}/2},$ shown above." :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo determine the critical points, we solve ${f}^{′}=0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}6-2y,\\,-2x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}6-2y=0 \\\\ -2x=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=3\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us a single point $(0,3).$ Since the point $(0,3)$ does not belong to the closed region of $D,$ we do not include it in the list of candidates for extrema.\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize each part of the boundary:\n\n- The line connecting $(-2,0)$ and $(2,0)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nr=⟨t,0⟩,\\,t\\in (-2,2).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on this boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(t,0) \\\\  & =6(t)-2(t)(0) \\\\  & =6t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the derivative of $F(t)$ is constant. So, $F(t)$ does not have critical points in the open interval $t\\in (-2,2).$\n\n- The parabola connecting $(-2,0)$ and $(2,0)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨t,2-\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}⟩,\\,t\\in (-2,2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(t,2-\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =6(t)-2(t)(2-\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}) \\\\  & ={t}^{3}+2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (-2,2),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ ({t}^{3}+2t)^{′} & =0 \\\\ 3{t}^{2}+2 & =0 \\\\ {t}^{2} & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation has no solution inside the interval $t\\in (-2,2).$ So, $F(t)$ does not have critical points in the parabola connecting $(-2,0)$ and $(2,0).$\n\nFinally, we have to include the corner points of the region as candidates for extrema. So, in total we obtain two candidates:\n\n$$\n(-2,0),\\,(2,0)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(-2,0)$ | $(2,0)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $-12$ | $12$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-12$ at the point $(-2,0)$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $12$ at the point $(2,0).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-86712/ma-source-field/316d1813f8cd52caa135b1a4ad50d41be0b5b5ff23dd6905bb682694f02b2c68"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
