 {:db/id "q-86722/ma-source-field/9289a74903bfe1cd9eb1b9543328bd51aa5c3e6535e128fc3984b753e9c2ae2f" :answer-field/id #uuid "20a29c76-a92d-5a15-9982-689804fc2dba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-86722/ma-source-field/9289a74903bfe1cd9eb1b9543328bd51aa5c3e6535e128fc3984b753e9c2ae2f/2d097c6098f80f52ee5ba9435064eca19bf6e687390453c16224718cf248b46c" "q-86722/ma-source-field/9289a74903bfe1cd9eb1b9543328bd51aa5c3e6535e128fc3984b753e9c2ae2f/2e6260515b6fa463c6e4c8f727c1947c360e4fe549f6f0a2563bf331dff2a408" "q-86722/ma-source-field/9289a74903bfe1cd9eb1b9543328bd51aa5c3e6535e128fc3984b753e9c2ae2f/1819542aabd20b79033235504b0e7212012a7a79579be9b26776ae71b88b4d78" "q-86722/ma-source-field/9289a74903bfe1cd9eb1b9543328bd51aa5c3e6535e128fc3984b753e9c2ae2f/c5823a9f57ac79b0aad7650330d01086f16a673c5cb9f3756a4e68d6df8acb2a" "q-86722/ma-source-field/9289a74903bfe1cd9eb1b9543328bd51aa5c3e6535e128fc3984b753e9c2ae2f/25c2dd989748ff13547c1b50aff8b511fc629a3f9f34a034b3bbd4198106a6e6"] :answer-field/correct "q-86722/ma-source-field/9289a74903bfe1cd9eb1b9543328bd51aa5c3e6535e128fc3984b753e9c2ae2f/1819542aabd20b79033235504b0e7212012a7a79579be9b26776ae71b88b4d78" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-86722" :question/id #uuid "8392959c-a393-5152-9307-9f96b5859277" :question/math-academy-id "q-86722" :question/problem "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)=1-4xy$ on the closed elliptical region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}\\le 9}.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$  \n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo do that, we solve ${f}^{′}=0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}-4y,\\,-4x\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-4y=0 \\\\ -4x=0\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us a single point $(0,0)\\in D,$ which we include in the list of candidates for the extrema.\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the boundary points that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize the bounding ellipse ${x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}=9.$ This gives\n\n$$\nr=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =1-4(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)(\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =1-18\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =1-9\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,2\\pi ),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (1-9\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))^{′} & =0 \\\\ -18\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation gives four solutions, namely\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\in (0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nWe also have to check the endpoint of the interval $t=0.$ Therefore, the only values of $t$ that can give rise to extreme values are \n\n$$\nt=0,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain five candidate points:\n\n$$\n(3,0),\\,(\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}),\\,(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}),\\,(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}),\\,(\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4})\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(3,0)$ | $(\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4})$ | $(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4})$ | $(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4})$ | $(\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $1$ | $1$ | $-8$ | $10$ | $-8$ | $10$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-8$ at the points $(\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4})$ and $(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $10$ at the points $(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4})$ and $(\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-86722/ma-source-field/9289a74903bfe1cd9eb1b9543328bd51aa5c3e6535e128fc3984b753e9c2ae2f"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
