 {:db/id "q-86724/ma-source-field/bbcf6a009f48640a7122095c34527ec02ec41eada25922a01552c7dfc6ddf0ca" :answer-field/id #uuid "6009c72a-9f51-5ad2-ae87-7dc2fbcb6754" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["q-86724/ma-source-field/bbcf6a009f48640a7122095c34527ec02ec41eada25922a01552c7dfc6ddf0ca/09074d944e366ffa3d909641449040bfc0cf5cbf14a228373bcb4fac64a62d68" "q-86724/ma-source-field/bbcf6a009f48640a7122095c34527ec02ec41eada25922a01552c7dfc6ddf0ca/2aa48fe82edf93230b8946896f4da64f15b2f4849272db6c0d27b650bfcd65ed" "q-86724/ma-source-field/bbcf6a009f48640a7122095c34527ec02ec41eada25922a01552c7dfc6ddf0ca/b0fae572d48614a37994e28adcd91c296fe6d6b652511a0af8fe5f205654d6a2" "q-86724/ma-source-field/bbcf6a009f48640a7122095c34527ec02ec41eada25922a01552c7dfc6ddf0ca/4613ed70822021d53d18cb81e433c09a05771d8e1156223f924c0b82119c8d55" "q-86724/ma-source-field/bbcf6a009f48640a7122095c34527ec02ec41eada25922a01552c7dfc6ddf0ca/afae609d80363c147107c378839f5932b6aa83b5ada09fd0fe18bb5a60ba918d"] :answer-field/correct "q-86724/ma-source-field/bbcf6a009f48640a7122095c34527ec02ec41eada25922a01552c7dfc6ddf0ca/4613ed70822021d53d18cb81e433c09a05771d8e1156223f924c0b82119c8d55" :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]}
 {:db/id "q-86724" :question/id #uuid "24be252f-8729-57e6-8082-158ca3588445" :question/math-academy-id "q-86724" :question/problem "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-6x+{y}^{2}+1$ on the closed region  $D={(x,y):{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4}.$ You are given that $f$ has a critical point at  $(3,0)$ and on the boundary of the region the points that could give rise to extreme values are  $(-2,0)$, and $(2,0).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$ \n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points inside the region $D.$ \n\nTo determine the critical points, let's first sketch the region:\n\n![](images/75/752c84f5ab0fb122102629e9ceada21c1bba0b8f0e93b3fffe0639e4284badab.png)\n\nSince the critical point $(3,0)$ does not lie inside $D,$ we do not include that point in the list of candidates for the extrema.\n\n**Step 2.** Find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values.\n\nIn this case, we are *given* the points on the boundary of our region that could potentially be global extrema for $f.$ These are\n\n$$\n(-2,0),\\,(2,0).\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2,0) & =(-2)^{2}-6(-2)+(0)^{2}+1 \\\\  & =17 \\\\ f(2,0) & =(2)^{2}-6(2)+(0)^{2}+1 \\\\  & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-7$ at the point $(2,0),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $17$ at the point $(-2,0).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-86724/ma-source-field/bbcf6a009f48640a7122095c34527ec02ec41eada25922a01552c7dfc6ddf0ca"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
