 {:db/id "q-87289" :question/id #uuid "94a980d8-2b2e-5cf9-9a95-55c26c4f6102" :question/math-academy-id "q-87289" :question/problem "Given $P(A)=\\frac{1}{4},$ $P(B)=\\frac{3}{10},$ and $P(A\\cup B)=\\frac{19}{40},$ determine whether $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive events." :question/worked-solution "To check if $A$ and $B$ are mutually exclusive, we must check that $P(A\\cap B)=0.$\n\nWe can calculate $P(A\\cap B)$ using the addition law:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A\\cup B) & =P(A)+P(B)-P(A\\cap B) \\\\ \\frac{19}{40} & =\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{3}{10}-P(A\\cap B) \\\\ P(A\\cap B) & =\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{3}{10}-\\frac{19}{40} \\\\  & =\\frac{10+12-19}{40} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{40} \\\\  & \\ne 0\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $A$ and $B$ are not mutually exclusive events." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-87289/ma-source-field/99d6d0ee8edba10392f436a3c5df138e10a745314cb4e9beb70459ef8320378d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]}
